thousands of protesters in dozens of  cities across russia have taken to the  streets holding up signs and chanting no  to war  russian police have already detained  thousands of people and the government  has been accused of attempting to squash  opposition to moscow's invasion of  ukraine  so how are russians mobilizing in an  increasingly authoritarian environment  and what are the consequences of  speaking up against putin's war joining  us to discuss this is leonid drapkin he  is coordinator of ovd info an  independent human rights media project  that helps the victims of political  persecution in russia thank you so much  for joining us on upfront leonid uh your  organization ovd info has documented  russian police officers beating and  kicking anti-war demonstrators slamming  a man's head on the roof of a car and  even allegedly using a stun gun on the  director of a local red cross branch can  you talk to us about the situation on  the ground and what abuses  you've seen in recent days  so the thing is that our government  unfortunately violates human rights  and people who just  go on the streets uh they are getting  detained and uh well these detentions  itself is a big violation of human  rights and uh i mean we are we're mad  about this and this is not what we like  and in addition to this  they do it in a very brutal way  and we see that the process of detection  itself  is very  brutal and sometimes police kicks  protesters sometimes they use special  special  things to to elector things just to make  them go to a police bus and then when  they are got to a police station  police use violence both uh  psychological and physical in order to  make protesters to  uh  to write uh  the things that they want police want in  their protocols of the detention so that  to make a court  in future to  uh to  place a right decision that police wants  and to find these protesters or to give  them arrests  you talk about arrests and detentions  that's a very important uh subject  between february 24th and february 27th  russian police arrested nearly 6 000  people can you tell us what happens when  a protester is detained and what they  have to go through what consequences  they face  uh the thing is is that this number  increases uh in uh just every every hour  and since russia is a very big country  and we have a lot of time zones and  people go on the streets in  all the cities of russia we see the  tensions like every hour and i can say  that i just got a new information that  as of now  it is not six but seven thousand people  who were detained over the last uh seven  days and it's not about uh one  particular day or one particular uh city  this is very widespread detentions in  many cities of russians of russia over  100 cities we know that there were  detentions over the last week  and yeah after being detained a lot of  people got arrests i i can say roughly  roughly it's five to ten percent of  those who were detained they got  arrested for uh several days for up to  15 days  and uh yeah like i mean those who were  protesting uh a week ago when russia  invaded to ukraine there  they can now be  in a jail and  what are they  charged with right when you go from the  detention to the formal arrest  what are they saying the protester or  the person did  the thing is that  russian  legislation is very repressive and  according to russian law in order to  to hold the rally you need to agree on  it with the municipal municipal power  you need to get a permission from our  authorities  and  you need to send a special well requests  and in most cases  uh of course our  authorities do not accept these requests  they prohibit this public rallies  and if you go on the street without this  permission it means that you violate you  you violate a special law uh when which  states that you are not allowed to take  part in public rally and this is the  reason and according to this law you can  get a fine if you violate it for the  first time and but if you violate it for  the second time you got not only fine  but also arrest for up to 15 or even 30  days in some cases a 60 year old  protester her name was tatiana told ovd  info that these anti-war protests were  the first she had attended since the  dissolution of the soviet union back in  1991.  she said it's scary of course but you  have to go out because people are dying  she said if we don't go out now how will  we look our children in the eyes later  why are these protests so significant in  this moment in time  uh i think that uh  so what we see now  it's like uh the most important uh event  in russian  history since the fall of soviet union i  guess and maybe even in more years and  the thing is that people are now dying  every day in ukraine and nobody knows  how many people died it's it's  definitely thousands of people  but nobody knows how many exactly and  so we need to well people things  think who go to protest who  publish some posts against the war they  they just try to stop it i mean nobody  really cares who will win the war  because there is no winner in any war  and in this war particularly because  like our nations are quite so we have  mutual history with ukraine and now it's  like we are killing each other this is  just insane and everyone thinks that  they should do something in order to  prevent uh further killings but um is  there something different though uh  leona is there something different about  this  uh moment though i mean there are people  who didn't protest last year there are  people who didn't protest 10 years ago  uh  is are things changing is the tide  turning right now  yeah the thing  you're right uh there are a lot of new  people who are coming on the streets who  is like the first  first rally in their life because  this topic  really  touches everyone because it's not about  really your political views but  many russians have relatives and friends  in ukraine and this is just uh a  personal personal 